      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          A great paint brush is the difference between professional and amateur results. The same paint, applied with a cheap throwaway brush, leaves visible brush marks, dropped bristles in the finish, and uneven lines. Applied with a quality brush, it lays down smooth, clean, and finished. The brush matters more than the paint.
        </p>

        <div id="brush-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Paint Brush Types: The Choices That Matter
          </h2>
          <p>
            Paint brushes vary along three dimensions that determine how the brush will perform: bristle material, brush shape, and brush size. Understanding each dimension helps you choose the right brush for your specific work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bristle Material: Synthetic vs Natural</h3>
          <p>
            Synthetic bristles (nylon, polyester, or blends) work with water-based paints, modern oil-based paints, stains, and most coatings. The synthetic materials don't absorb water like natural bristles do, so they maintain shape and stiffness in water-based products where natural brushes would go limp. The best synthetic brushes (Purdy Clearcut Glide, Wooster Silver Tip) are also fully capable on oil-based products.
          </p>
          <p>
            Natural bristles (China bristle from pig hair, ox hair, badger hair) are optimized for oil-based products. The keratin structure of natural hair interacts with oil-based paints to produce smoother release than synthetic bristles can achieve. Natural bristles cannot be used with water-based products — the water makes them swell, lose shape, and become permanently ruined for future work. Natural-bristle brushes are specialty tools for users who apply oil-based polyurethane, varnish, marine paint, or oil enamel regularly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brush Shape: Flat vs Angled vs Round</h3>
          <p>
            Flat brushes have bristles that meet at a straight edge across the entire width. They're best for broad surfaces — large trim, doors, and walls where you don't need to follow a precise edge. The standard 3-inch flat is the workhorse for wide cut-in applications.
          </p>
          <p>
            Angled sash brushes have bristles cut at a diagonal so that one corner of the brush comes to a sharp point. The angled tip allows precision cut-in along corners, edges, and tight spaces. The 2.5-inch angled sash is the single most versatile brush shape — appropriate for almost any cut-in, trim, or detail work.
          </p>
          <p>
            Round brushes are specialty tools for spindles, decorative profiles, and fine artistic work. They're not commonly used in residential painting but have specific applications in furniture refinishing and detailed trim work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brush Size: Matching Width to Work</h3>
          <p>
            Brush width affects paint capacity, coverage rate, and precision. Wider brushes carry more paint and cover faster but offer less precision. Narrower brushes offer better precision and control but slow coverage. The general rule is to use the largest brush that maintains adequate precision for the work — a 3-inch flat on broad walls, 2.5-inch angled sash for general cut-in and trim, 1-inch detail brush for window mullions and narrow profiles.
          </p>

