        <div id="how-to-brush" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Brush Paint Like a Pro
          </h2>
          <p>
            The technique of brushing paint is learnable in an hour of practice but takes years to truly master. The most common amateur mistakes have specific fixes that dramatically improve finish quality immediately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Load the Brush Properly</h3>
          <p>
            Dip the brush into paint to about one-third the bristle length — not deeper, and definitely not all the way to the ferrule. Tap (don't drag) the brush gently against the side of the can to remove excess. The goal is a brush that's full but not dripping. Brushes loaded too heavily drip and produce drips; brushes loaded too lightly run out of paint quickly and create lap marks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Cut In with the Angled Tip</h3>
          <p>
            For cut-in work along edges, use the chisel-tipped corner of an angled sash brush. Hold the brush like a pencil, with the sharp angle toward the edge you're following. Make a confident, single stroke along the edge — multiple tentative strokes produce wavy lines. With practice, you can cut in along ceiling lines and trim edges without using painter's tape.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Lay Down Paint, Then Tip Out</h3>
          <p>
            For broader brushed surfaces (doors, large trim, walls), use a two-phase technique: first, load the brush and apply paint to the surface in roughly the right area, using multiple loaded passes to cover the section. Then, with a lightly-loaded brush, make long parallel strokes (called 'tipping out') to smooth the paint and eliminate visible brush marks. The tipping pass is the difference between obvious brush marks and a near-smooth finish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Maintain a Wet Edge</h3>
          <p>
            Like rolling, brushing requires maintaining a wet edge throughout the work. Never let one section of paint dry before brushing into it from the next section. Dry edges produce visible lap marks where the dry and wet meet. Plan your work to finish complete sections — entire wall, entire door panel, entire trim run — before stopping.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Brush in the Direction of the Grain</h3>
          <p>
            For wood surfaces, always brush in the direction of the wood grain on the final pass. Cross-grain brushing leaves visible texture even after the paint dries. On smooth surfaces like drywall, brush direction matters less but consistency still produces a more polished look — pick a direction and stay with it across the entire section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Don't Over-Brush</h3>
          <p>
            The single most common amateur mistake is over-brushing — going back over already-tacky paint with the brush in an attempt to 'fix' something. This pulls the partially-cured paint, creating drag marks, brush ridges, and a worse finish than would have resulted from leaving it alone. Once paint starts to skin over (typically 5-10 minutes after application), do not brush it again. Let it dry and address any issues on the next coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brush-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Brush Care: Making Quality Brushes Last
          </h2>
          <p>
            A quality brush ($15-25) cleaned and stored properly will last 5-10 years of regular use. The same brush dried with paint in the bristles or stored badly will be ruined within a single project. Proper care extends the lifetime cost-per-use of a quality brush dramatically below disposable alternatives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Clean Immediately After Use</h3>
          <p>
            The single most important practice is cleaning the brush immediately after finishing the work — not at the end of the day, not after lunch, not 'in a minute.' Paint that starts to cure inside the bristles is extremely difficult to remove, and once it has cured the brush is essentially ruined. Set up a cleaning station before starting the work so the brush can go directly from work into cleaning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cleaning Water-Based Paints</h3>
          <p>
            Rinse the brush under warm running water while working the bristles with your fingers to release paint. Continue until the water runs completely clear, paying particular attention to the area where the bristles meet the ferrule — paint that accumulates here is the most common reason brushes harden and lose flexibility. Use a brush comb (about $5) to remove paint from deep within the bristles. Spin the brush gently to remove excess water (a brush spinner tool is helpful here).
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cleaning Oil-Based Paints</h3>
          <p>
            For oil-based paints, rinse the brush in mineral spirits in a container, working the bristles to release paint. Switch to fresh mineral spirits one or two more times until the spirits stay clear. Wash the brush with warm soapy water to remove the mineral spirits residue. Dispose of used mineral spirits responsibly at a hazardous waste collection site, not down the drain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Dry with Bristles Aligned</h3>
          <p>
            After cleaning, comb the bristles into their original aligned position and let the brush dry hanging by the handle (with bristles pointing down) or laying flat on a clean surface. Never store a brush wet with the bristles compressed or bent — they'll dry in that position and the brush will never recover. The original cardboard keeper that comes with most quality brushes is designed to maintain proper bristle alignment during drying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Store Properly</h3>
          <p>
            Store clean, dry brushes hanging from a peg or in their original keeper. Avoid storing brushes loose in a drawer where the bristles can bend and deform. Never store a wet brush. If you'll use the brush again the next day, you can wrap it in plastic to keep it from drying out, but for any longer break, clean it thoroughly first.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mb-2">Brush Care Quick Reference</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-green-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Clean immediately after every use — never leave paint to cure in bristles</li>
              <li>• Pay special attention to the ferrule area where paint accumulates</li>
              <li>• Use a brush comb to release paint from deep within bristles</li>
              <li>• Dry with bristles aligned in their original position</li>
              <li>• Store hanging or in original keeper, never compressed in a drawer</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

