          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            How We Tested: 14 Brushes, 6 Months, Real Projects
          </h2>
          <p>
            Our brush testing was designed to expose real-world performance over time rather than out-of-the-box impressions. Each candidate brush was used for at least 8 hours of actual painting work across multiple project types: interior wall cut-in, baseboard and crown molding, cabinet doors with high-gloss enamel, window sashes with multiple panes, exterior trim painting, and stained or sealed wood trim.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each brush was evaluated on six dimensions: paint capacity (how much paint a single dip holds), release smoothness (how cleanly the paint releases without skipping or flooding), edge holding (how well the chisel-tipped corner maintains its sharp angle), finish quality (visible brush marks after the paint dries), control (precision when cutting in tight to edges), and durability (performance after dozens of cleanings and weeks of use).
          </p>
          <p>
            Brushes were cleaned identically after each use following manufacturer recommendations, stored hanging in original keepers, and re-evaluated periodically through the test period. The brushes that ended the test in the best condition — still cutting clean lines, still holding their shape, still releasing paint smoothly — earned the recommendations in this guide. Brushes that lost their edge, shed bristles, or developed bent or flagged bristles within the test period did not make the list regardless of their initial performance.
          </p>
          <p>
            The clear conclusion from the test: there is a meaningful performance gap between quality brushes ($15-25) and budget brushes ($3-8), and the gap widens with use over time. Budget brushes can produce acceptable results when new but degrade quickly; quality brushes maintain their performance through years of regular use. For any DIY painter doing more than a single project, the higher upfront cost of quality brushes is repaid quickly in better finish quality and longer brush life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Buying Tips: How to Recognize Brush Quality at the Store
          </h2>
          <p>
            Not all expensive brushes are great, and not every great brush carries a premium price tag. Knowing how to evaluate brush quality at the store helps you avoid paying for marketing rather than performance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start by examining the bristles. Premium brushes have bristles of varying lengths within the brush — the technical term is 'tipped and flagged' bristles, where the ends of the bristles are split (flagged) to create more surface area for paint pickup. Cheap brushes have uniform, blunt-cut bristles that hold less paint and release it less smoothly. Run your fingers across the bristle ends; quality brushes feel soft and slightly fluffy at the tip, while cheap brushes feel uniformly stiff.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pull gently on the bristles. A quality brush should not shed any bristles when you tug — the bristles are firmly set into the ferrule with epoxy or similar adhesive. Cheap brushes will shed bristles immediately, and those bristles will end up in your finish. Watch for any loose bristles falling out as you check.
          </p>
          <p>
            Examine the ferrule (the metal band that connects the bristles to the handle). Quality brushes use stainless steel or copper-plated ferrules that won't corrode and stain the paint. Cheap brushes use uncoated steel that develops rust over time, especially after exposure to water. The ferrule should also be tightly clamped onto the bristles without any visible gaps where paint could enter and cause early failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the handle. Wood handles should be smooth, well-finished, and properly shaped for comfort. The handle should fit your grip naturally during the painting motion you'll use. Some painters prefer thicker handles for general work and slimmer handles for detail work; both styles are available in quality brushes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, check the brush packaging. Most quality brushes come with a cardboard sleeve or 'keeper' that protects the bristles during transport and storage. This is also useful long-term storage equipment, so save it. Cheap brushes typically come without a keeper or with a flimsy plastic wrap that's not useful for storage.
          </p>
        </div>

