            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Synthetic or natural bristle — which should I use?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Synthetic with water-based paints, natural bristle (China bristle) with oil-based products, varnishes, and polyurethanes. Water makes natural bristles swell and lose shape. The best modern synthetics like Purdy Clearcut Glide handle both water and oil-based products effectively.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What size brush should I buy?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A 2.5-inch angled sash brush is the most versatile size for general work. Add a 1-inch detail brush for windows and tight spaces, and a 3-inch flat for broad work. Cabinet refinishers should also have a 2-inch short-handle brush.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a paint brush last?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A quality brush cared for properly lasts 5-10 years. Cheap brushes typically last 1-2 projects. The lifetime cost-per-use of a quality brush is significantly lower despite the higher upfront cost.</p>
